            "The most prolific mathematician in history",
            "Contributed to virtually every branch of mathematics: calculus, number theory, graph theory, topology, mechanics, optics, astronomy. Introduced most modern notation: f(x), e, i, pi, sigma notation. Published over 800 papers.",
            "He was so productive that journals were still publishing his backlog 50 years after his death. He could do enormous calculations in his head. Euler's identity e^(i*pi)+1=0 unites the five most important constants in one equation -- often called the most beautiful equation in mathematics.",
            "Went blind in both eyes but his productivity INCREASED. He dictated papers from memory, producing more mathematics blind than most produce sighted.", 10, 10, 10, 7),

        new("Gauss", "1777-1855", "Germany", "enlightenment", "#7B68EE",
            "The Prince of Mathematics",
            "Number theory (quadratic reciprocity, prime number theorem conjecture), non-Euclidean geometry (which he kept secret), statistics (Gaussian distribution), magnetism, astronomy (predicted Ceres' orbit). Proved the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra.",
            "He was making groundbreaking discoveries as a teenager. At 19, he proved that a regular 17-gon could be constructed with compass and straightedge -- a problem open for 2,000 years. He kept many discoveries unpublished, so others \"rediscovered\" his work decades later.",
            "When told someone had independently discovered non-Euclidean geometry, he replied he'd known it for decades but feared publishing because it was too radical. Multiple mathematicians were devastated to discover Gauss had beaten them years earlier.", 10, 10, 9, 6),

        new("Galois", "1811-1832", "France", "modern", "#E74C3C",
            "Died at 20, changed mathematics forever",
            "Invented group theory and Galois theory, proving that there is NO general formula for solving polynomial equations of degree 5 or higher. Established the connection between field extensions and group symmetry.",
            "He solved a 300-year-old problem (the insolvability of the quintic) by inventing an entirely new field of mathematics. His work connects algebra to symmetry in a way that underlies all of modern abstract algebra and much of physics. He did this as a <strong>teenager</strong>.",
            "Wrote his mathematical testament the night before a duel (over a woman or politics -- historians debate). He was killed the next morning at age 20. His scribbled notes, annotated \"I have no time,\" launched group theory.", 10, 10, 6, 10),

        new("Riemann", "1826-1866", "Germany", "modern", "#3498DB",
            "Reshaped geometry and number theory",
            "Created Riemannian geometry (curved spaces of any dimension), which Einstein later used for general relativity. Posed the Riemann Hypothesis about the zeros of the zeta function -- the most famous unsolved problem in mathematics.",
            "His 1854 lecture on the foundations of geometry literally gave Einstein the mathematical language to describe curved spacetime 60 years later. Without Riemann, no general relativity. The Riemann Hypothesis, if proven, would reveal the deep structure of prime numbers.",
